\nValidation: 145 tests, 2335 assertions, zero errors and only the expected\\nclose-out gate failure. Regression suites pass; kondo has zero errors and\\nno new warnings.\\nTXT\\n git diff --cached --check\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793296786225-reviewer-hardening2.glm-5.3.stdout.log:42:- `clj -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test` → **23 tests, 997 assertions, 1 failure, 0 errors** — the single failure is the expected close-out gate; the live recount printed `{:pass 53, :blocked 2, :mismatch 2}, :fixture-count 57, :manifest-bindings 155, :function-arity-candidates 175` with 143 unexercised, matching slice9_evidence.edn and the md; the checked-in evidence was not rewritten by the run.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793392000000-architect-adopt-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md && cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-30 20:08:44 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-31 03:08:44 +07\\n\\nThird confirmation round, narrowly scoped to the two P2s I raised in r2, against fix commit `12feb7d1` (`git diff abbfb998..12feb7d1`, re-read from the diff, not assumed). All probes re-ran read-only on the JVM lane through the test namespace's own runners (`run-outcome`, `run-profile-outcome`, `walker-run`, `semantic-run`, `outcome-ast`) plus `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` for the eligibility arms; no source files edited, nothing committed. Probe-honesty note: my r2 report quoted the probes with a bare `raise` operand; this round established that source-spelled fn/defn values are descriptor **maps**, not applicable values, in this lane — the verdict-bearing probes below therefore spell raise exactly the way the corpus and the compiler's own bridges do (an applicable `probe/note` host splice / raw lam), and the source-spelled-raise behavior is reported separately as informational.\\n\\n## N1 — invoke/invoke-with admission bypass: CLOSED\\n\\nRuling (kimi r5): witness-gate both operators at the compile branch like `yang.call/eligible?`; qualified `:yang.call/not-callable` for every unadmitted callee; no shared-runtime change. Verified in place: the new branch at `clojure.cljc:1298-1318` sits before the `native-name?` fall-through, checks `dsg__admitted?` on `dsg__invocation_candidate`, routes the success arm through the shared runtime and the refusal arm through `yang.call/transfer` + `condition :yang.call/not-callable` + callee evidence. `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` is untouched by the fix (diff lists only the manifests, `clojure.cljc`, and tests) — the no-shared-runtime-change ruling is honored.\\n\\nForged-callee probes, both operators (r2 baseline → now; raise = `probe/note` splice):\\n\\n```\\n:probe-copied-invoke       [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]   ; r2: [:yin.k/ok 42] — the forged callee RAN\\n:probe-copied-invoke-with  [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]\\n:probe-scalar-invoke       [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]                    ; r2: raw host \\\"Cannot apply non-function\\\"\\n:probe-scalar-invoke-with  [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]\\n:probe-guestfn-invoke      [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {…full guest-fn descriptor…}}}}]  ; r2: raw host defect\\n:probe-guestfn-invoke-with (same qualified refusal)\\n:probe-positive-both       [:yin.k/ok [42 42]]                                                                                                            ; admitted f through both operators still works\\n```\\n\\nDynamic eligibility via `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` (sanctioned profile construction), walker and semantic lanes:\\n\\n```\\n:elig true  :walker/:semantic  :invoke [:yin.k/ok :called]  :invoke-with [:yin.k/ok :called]  :operator [:yin.k/ok true]\\n:elig false :walker/:semantic  :invoke [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data {:callee callable}}]  :invoke-with (same)  :operator [:yin.k/ok false]\\n```\\n\\nAlias route (the sharp edge I went hunting for after a value-position probe returned 42): `(def g yang.call/invoke)` then calling through `g` resolves to the gated operator at compile time, so the forged callee still refuses:\\n\\n```\\n:alias-forged-invoke    [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]\\n:alias-forged-scalar    [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]\\n:alias-admitted         [:yin.k/ok 42]              ; the earlier 42 — admitted callee through the same gate\\n:valueposition-eligible [:yin.k/ok [false true]]    ; (def p yang.call/eligible?): forged h → false, admitted f → true\\n```\\n\\n**P3 | informational, pre-existing, non-blocking | source-spelled raise operands are not callable values.** `(fn [c] c)` or `(defn raise …)` in the raise operand compiles to a descriptor map (`compile-av-fn` → `compile-call-descriptor`, clojure.cljc:1116-1118); the shared runtime's `(if (data/callable? raise) … (raise …))` else-branch then applies the value it just decided is not callable (call.cljc:118,142) → host `{:reason :not-applicable, :kind :map}` \\\"Cannot apply non-function\\\". This crashes the **positive control identically** and `call.cljc` is byte-identical at `abbfb998`, so it is a pre-existing property of the surface (the invoke raise operand is profile-mediated: host fns and raw lams — the fixtures' `profile-raise` splices and the compiler's own bridges at clojure.cljc:1194,1234 — are the spellings that have ever worked), not a regression from `12feb7d1`; the new refusal path inherits the same class only for raise spellings that never worked anywhere on this surface. Recorded for the architect's list (the non-callable else-branch applying its own raise is the odd part); does not gate sign-off under r5, which pinned \\\"no change to `yang.core.call` is required\\\". Same class: under-arity `(yang.call/invoke h [21])` (below the branch's `(<= 4 …)` guard, pre-fix compilation unchanged) → host defect; over-arity tolerated (42) — both identical in class at the parent commit.\\n\\n## N2 — closure manifests missing call.cljc: CLOSED\\n\\nRuling: mechanical regeneration. All three manifests now list `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` (verified in the diff and by grep), counts updated to 156/154/155 files, 148 shared, union 168. Gate test re-run:\\n\\n```\\n$ clojure -M:test -n dao.host.gate-test\\nRan 14 tests containing 178 assertions.\\n0 failures, 0 errors.\\n```\\n\\n`independent-traversal-agrees-with-the-committed-closure` (red on all three lanes in r2), `closure-totals-are-the-design-totals`, and `gate-passes-on-the-current-tree` are all among the 14 green.\\n\\n## Suite re-verification (JVM, exact counts)\\n\\n- `yang.clojure-test` **86 tests / 784 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** — grew from r2's 85/763 by the new `test-common-call-invocation-source-admission` fixture and the extended profile-eligibility/source-admission arms; green.\\n- `yang.core.call-test` **4/13**, `yin.vm.macro-test` **25/188**, `yang.clojure.stream-eval-test` **13/309** — all 0 failures, identical to r2's counts (shared runtime untouched, as ruled).\\n- Required-four total: **128 tests / 1294 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\\n- Kondo could not be re-run (`clojure -M:kondo` approval denied in this sandbox, as in r2) — unverified by me.\\n\\n## CLOSED/OPEN table\\n\\n| Item | Ruling | Verdict |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| N1 invoke/invoke-with witness bypass | kimi r5: gate both operators at the compile branch | **CLOSED** (all forged shapes refuse with the qualified condition through both operators, incl. the def-alias route; positive control and both eligibility arms intact on both lanes) |\\n| N2 closure manifests missing call.cljc | mechanical regeneration | **CLOSED** (gate test 14/178 green; manifests + totals updated) |\\n| P3 raise-spelling surface oddity | informational, pre-existing at abbfb998 | noted for the architect, non-blocking |\\n\\n## Sign-off readiness\\n\\n**Ready for Architect sign-off.** Both P2s from r2 are closed with probe evidence at `12feb7d1`: the protocol's front door now refuses every unadmitted callee with the qualified `:yang.call/not-callable` condition through both operators (including the alias route), genuinely admitted callees — static and dynamic-eligibility — still work on both lanes, the shared runtime is untouched, and the closure gate test is green with the manifests regenerated. The one P3 (source-spelled raise operands being non-callable values, a pre-existing property that affects admitted callees identically and predates this fix) is recorded for the architect's backlog and does not gate this adoption.\\n\\n---\\n\\nExecution summary: probes ran as one-line `clojure -M -e` evaluations against the loaded test namespace (fully-qualified var references; each probe individually exception-guarded); suites via `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. No repo files modified, nothing committed. The findings file `collab/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md` could **not** be written — permission denied, exactly as in r2 — so this response is the deliverable; both the r2 and r3 reports now await capturing into collab/ by a session with write access.\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\n```\\n\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\n\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\n\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\n\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\n\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\n\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\n\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\navailable. Do not run another lane set concurrently on the laptop.\\n\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\n\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\n\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\n\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\n\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\n\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\n5-6-hour run.\\n\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\n\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\n\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\n  The assertion \\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\n\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\nchanges.\\\"\\n\\nTwo tiers, by purpose:\\n\\n- **The nightly full suite** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on shiva, tmux\\n  session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the orchestrator's\\n  automation): every lane, every host target, against origin/master —\\n  the JVM full suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its\\n  results are a post-hoc integrity net, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\n- **Branch/worktree testing**: only the namespaces that target the\\n  changed code (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff touches),\\n  one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node) when cljc\\n  goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch: a branch\\n  gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree re-proven.\\n\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\n\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling):** before any merge\\ninto master, the `dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is\\nwhat everything is built on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the\\nsubsystem's tests, `--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded\\nsurfaces) is part of every branch gate regardless of which subsystems\\nthe diff's own changes select, because a change can break the foundation\\nwithout touching it. A red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or\\ndiagnosed as pre-existing by the owning seat.\\n\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\n\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\nchanges\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\"it should not be for\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\"\\n\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\n\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on\\n  shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\\n  orchestrator's automation; it fetches and checks out origin/master\\n  before anything runs). Every lane, every host target: the JVM full\\n  suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its results are a\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\n\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\n\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\n\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\n\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\n\\\"Patch does not apply\\\" or \\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\".\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\n\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\n\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\"run tests remotely\\nover SSH\\\" to \\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\": a heavy implementation or\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\n\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\nreference):\\n\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\"Get the code there\\\".\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\"$(mise env -s\\n   bash)\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\n   inside the same command.\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\n\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793392000000-architect-adopt-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:15:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '2000,2105p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && cat collab/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"                     (= 1 (count forms))\\n                     (compile-form head tail? (assoc env ::top-level true) macro-env)\\n                     ;; sequence\\n                     :else\\n                     (cond-> {:type     :application\\n                              :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['_]\\n                                         :body (go rest-forms macro-env)}\\n                              :operands [(discard-result\\n                                           (compile-form head false\\n                                                         (assoc env ::top-level true) macro-env))]}\\n                       tail? (assoc :tail? true))))))]\\n       (cond->> (go forms initial-macro-env)\\n         (outcomes? env) (u/let1\\n                           'dsg__admitted?\\n                           (u/sexp->uast\\n                             '(fn [dsg__candidate]\\n                                (if (data/map? dsg__candidate)\\n                                  (let [dsg__entry (dsg__get dsg__candidate :yang.call/entry)\\n                                        dsg__witness (dsg__get dsg__candidate :clj/admission)]\\n                                    (if (data/callable? dsg__entry)\\n                                      (dsg__equal\\n                                        dsg__witness\\n                                        (dsg__make_admission\\n                                          (dsg__assoc dsg__candidate :clj/admission nil)))\\n                                      false))\\n                                  false))))\\n         (outcomes? env) (u/let1 'dsg__make_entry (call-entry))\\n         (outcomes? env) (u/let1\\n                           'dsg__make_admission\\n                           (u/sexp->uast\\n                             '(fn [dsg__descriptor]\\n                                (fn [dsg__candidate]\\n                                  (if (data/callable? dsg__call_token)\\n                                    (dsg__get dsg__descriptor :yang.call/entry)\\n                                    nil)))))\\n         (outcomes? env) (u/then\\n                           (discard-result\\n                             (u/app (u/lam '[get = assoc empty?] call/uast)\\n                                    (u/v 'dsg__get) (u/v 'dsg__equal)\\n                                    (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/v 'dsg__empty))))\\n         (outcomes? env) (u/let1 'dsg__call_token (u/lam [] (u/lit nil)))\\n         ;; Guest closures keep captured environments portable: binding host\\n         ;; primitive values here would make UCF machine-data validation fail.\\n         ;; These helpers cover generated map lookups/writes and comparisons;\\n         ;; conj/vec are already protected by the convention's binder checks.\\n         ;; Evaluate siblings outside each other's bindings to avoid retaining\\n         ;; a chain of redundant helper environments.\\n         (outcomes? env) (#(u/app\\n                             (u/lam '[dsg__get dsg__equal dsg__assoc dsg__empty] %)\\n                             (if (contains? (::defined env) 'get)\\n                               (u/sexp->uast\\n                                 '(fn [m k]\\n                                    (if (data/map? m)\\n                                      (let [find (fn [self entries]\\n                                                   (if (data/content= 0 (data/count entries))\\n                                                     nil\\n                                                     (let [pair (data/nth entries 0)]\\n                                                       (if (data/content= k (data/nth pair 0))\\n                                                         (data/nth pair 1)\\n                                                         (self self (data/subvec entries 1))))))]\\n                                        (find find (vec m)))\\n                                      nil)))\\n                               (u/sexp->uast '(fn [m k] (get m k))))\\n                             (u/sexp->uast '(fn [a b] (data/content= a b)))\\n                             (u/sexp->uast\\n                               '(fn [m k v k2 v2 k3 v3]\\n                                  (let [one (conj m (conj [] k v))]\\n                                    (if (data/content= k2 nil)\\n                                      one\\n                                      (let [two (conj one (conj [] k2 v2))]\\n                                        (if (data/content= k3 nil)\\n                                          two\\n                                          (conj two (conj [] k3 v3))))))))\\n                             (u/sexp->uast '(fn [xs] (data/content= 0 (data/count xs))))))\\n         (outcomes? env) u/mark-tails)))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; The guest collection and higher-order library (slice 7)\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;;\\n;; Guest-side source, compiled through this compiler like any program: the\\n;; functions that invoke guest closures (map, filter, reduce, comp, partial)\\n;; must run on the VM, never on the host, because a closure is a VM value. It\\n;; is written for the argument-vector convention with the outcome model, so a\\n;; closure it calls is unwrapped by the compiler's own early-return pattern\\n;; and a closure's failure ends the library function with the same outcome.\\n;;\\n;; What is derived, from the VM primitives and the `data` module only:\\n;; identity, inc/dec, second, the collection predicates (coll?/vector?/map?/\\n;; set?/seq?/list?/sequential?, from the `portable/*` adapters of data/count,\\n;; peek, dissoc and disj: a refusal as data says \\\"not that kind\\\"), boolean?,\\n;; fn? (data/callable?), seq, rem, reverse, list, cons, vector, count,\\n;; contains?, rest, map/mapv/filter/remove/reduce (with reduced)/into/concat/merge,\\n;; keys/vals/disj and set union/difference,\\n;; comp, partial, gensym (explicit supply), not=, and `apply`, which is a\\n;; compiler intrinsic over the convention's argument vector.\\n;;\\n;; What stays a host primitive, named by the `kind` module: a symbol, a\\n;; keyword and a host string are distinguished or built only by the host\\n;; (symbol?, keyword?, name, symbol, keyword, str, int?), as is metadata access\\n;; (meta). No `data` export or\\n;; VM primitive tells a symbol from a keyword. var? and resolve are the host's\\n;; Var primitives: a harness on a host with Vars registers them in `kind`\\n;; (`kind-module` leaves them out); a program that never mentions them does\\n;; not need them.\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-30 20:08:44 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-31 03:08:44 +07\\n\\nThird confirmation round, narrowly scoped to the two P2s I raised in r2, against fix commit `12feb7d1` (`git diff abbfb998..12feb7d1`, re-read from the diff, not assumed). All probes re-ran read-only on the JVM lane through the test namespace's own runners (`run-outcome`, `run-profile-outcome`, `walker-run`, `semantic-run`, `outcome-ast`) plus `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` for the eligibility arms; no source files edited, nothing committed. Probe-honesty note: my r2 report quoted the probes with a bare `raise` operand; this round established that source-spelled fn/defn values are descriptor **maps**, not applicable values, in this lane — the verdict-bearing probes below therefore spell raise exactly the way the corpus and the compiler's own bridges do (an applicable `probe/note` host splice / raw lam), and the source-spelled-raise behavior is reported separately as informational.\\n\\n## N1 — invoke/invoke-with admission bypass: CLOSED\\n\\nRuling (kimi r5): witness-gate both operators at the compile branch like `yang.call/eligible?`; qualified `:yang.call/not-callable` for every unadmitted callee; no shared-runtime change. Verified in place: the new branch at `clojure.cljc:1298-1318` sits before the `native-name?` fall-through, checks `dsg__admitted?` on `dsg__invocation_candidate`, routes the success arm through the shared runtime and the refusal arm through `yang.call/transfer` + `condition :yang.call/not-callable` + callee evidence. `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` is untouched by the fix (diff lists only the manifests, `clojure.cljc`, and tests) — the no-shared-runtime-change ruling is honored.\\n\\nForged-callee probes, both operators (r2 baseline → now; raise = `probe/note` splice):\\n\\n```\\n:probe-copied-invoke       [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]   ; r2: [:yin.k/ok 42] — the forged callee RAN\\n:probe-copied-invoke-with  [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]\\n:probe-scalar-invoke       [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]                    ; r2: raw host \\\"Cannot apply non-function\\\"\\n:probe-scalar-invoke-with  [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]\\n:probe-guestfn-invoke      [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {…full guest-fn descriptor…}}}}]  ; r2: raw host defect\\n:probe-guestfn-invoke-with (same qualified refusal)\\n:probe-positive-both       [:yin.k/ok [42 42]]                                                                                                            ; admitted f through both operators still works\\n```\\n\\nDynamic eligibility via `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` (sanctioned profile construction), walker and semantic lanes:\\n\\n```\\n:elig true  :walker/:semantic  :invoke [:yin.k/ok :called]  :invoke-with [:yin.k/ok :called]  :operator [:yin.k/ok true]\\n:elig false :walker/:semantic  :invoke [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data {:callee callable}}]  :invoke-with (same)  :operator [:yin.k/ok false]\\n```\\n\\nAlias route (the sharp edge I went hunting for after a value-position probe returned 42): `(def g yang.call/invoke)` then calling through `g` resolves to the gated operator at compile time, so the forged callee still refuses:\\n\\n```\\n:alias-forged-invoke    [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]\\n:alias-forged-scalar    [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]\\n:alias-admitted         [:yin.k/ok 42]              ; the earlier 42 — admitted callee through the same gate\\n:valueposition-eligible [:yin.k/ok [false true]]    ; (def p yang.call/eligible?): forged h → false, admitted f → true\\n```\\n\\n**P3 | informational, pre-existing, non-blocking | source-spelled raise operands are not callable values.** `(fn [c] c)` or `(defn raise …)` in the raise operand compiles to a descriptor map (`compile-av-fn` → `compile-call-descriptor`, clojure.cljc:1116-1118); the shared runtime's `(if (data/callable? raise) … (raise …))` else-branch then applies the value it just decided is not callable (call.cljc:118,142) → host `{:reason :not-applicable, :kind :map}` \\\"Cannot apply non-function\\\". This crashes the **positive control identically** and `call.cljc` is byte-identical at `abbfb998`, so it is a pre-existing property of the surface (the invoke raise operand is profile-mediated: host fns and raw lams — the fixtures' `profile-raise` splices and the compiler's own bridges at clojure.cljc:1194,1234 — are the spellings that have ever worked), not a regression from `12feb7d1`; the new refusal path inherits the same class only for raise spellings that never worked anywhere on this surface. Recorded for the architect's list (the non-callable else-branch applying its own raise is the odd part); does not gate sign-off under r5, which pinned \\\"no change to `yang.core.call` is required\\\". Same class: under-arity `(yang.call/invoke h [21])` (below the branch's `(<= 4 …)` guard, pre-fix compilation unchanged) → host defect; over-arity tolerated (42) — both identical in class at the parent commit.\\n\\n## N2 — closure manifests missing call.cljc: CLOSED\\n\\nRuling: mechanical regeneration. All three manifests now list `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` (verified in the diff and by grep), counts updated to 156/154/155 files, 148 shared, union 168. Gate test re-run:\\n\\n```\\n$ clojure -M:test -n dao.host.gate-test\\nRan 14 tests containing 178 assertions.\\n0 failures, 0 errors.\\n```\\n\\n`independent-traversal-agrees-with-the-committed-closure` (red on all three lanes in r2), `closure-totals-are-the-design-totals`, and `gate-passes-on-the-current-tree` are all among the 14 green.\\n\\n## Suite re-verification (JVM, exact counts)\\n\\n- `yang.clojure-test` **86 tests / 784 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** — grew from r2's 85/763 by the new `test-common-call-invocation-source-admission` fixture and the extended profile-eligibility/source-admission arms; green.\\n- `yang.core.call-test` **4/13**, `yin.vm.macro-test` **25/188**, `yang.clojure.stream-eval-test` **13/309** — all 0 failures, identical to r2's counts (shared runtime untouched, as ruled).\\n- Required-four total: **128 tests / 1294 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\\n- Kondo could not be re-run (`clojure -M:kondo` approval denied in this sandbox, as in r2) — unverified by me.\\n\\n## CLOSED/OPEN table\\n\\n| Item | Ruling | Verdict |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| N1 invoke/invoke-with witness bypass | kimi r5: gate both operators at the compile branch | **CLOSED** (all forged shapes refuse with the qualified condition through both operators, incl. the def-alias route; positive control and both eligibility arms intact on both lanes) |\\n| N2 closure manifests missing call.cljc | mechanical regeneration | **CLOSED** (gate test 14/178 green; manifests + totals updated) |\\n| P3 raise-spelling surface oddity | informational, pre-existing at abbfb998 | noted for the architect, non-blocking |\\n\\n## Sign-off readiness\\n\\n**Ready for Architect sign-off.** Both P2s from r2 are closed with probe evidence at `12feb7d1`: the protocol's front door now refuses every unadmitted callee with the qualified `:yang.call/not-callable` condition through both operators (including the alias route), genuinely admitted callees — static and dynamic-eligibility — still work on both lanes, the shared runtime is untouched, and the closure gate test is green with the manifests regenerated. The one P3 (source-spelled raise operands being non-callable values, a pre-existing property that affects admitted callees identically and predates this fix) is recorded for the architect's backlog and does not gate this adoption.\\n\\n---\\n\\nExecution summary: probes ran as one-line `clojure -M -e` evaluations against the loaded test namespace (fully-qualified var references; each probe individually exception-guarded); suites via `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. No repo files modified, nothing committed. The findings file `collab/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md` could **not** be written — permission denied, exactly as in r2 — so this response is the deliverable; both the r2 and r3 reports now await capturing into collab/ by a session with write access.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793392000000-architect-adopt-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:24:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"cat collab/1793309600000-architect-yang-call-kimi-r4-arbitration.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md && sed -n '1,230p' collab/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"NONECompleted-GMT: 2026-10-30 19:18:22 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-31 02:18:22 +07\\n\\nConfirmation round against `abbfb998` (branch rebased onto `63674368`; everything re-read from `git diff 63674368..abbfb998`, not assumed from the pre-rebase review). I re-ran my own s1 probes for each arbitrated item, probed the eligibility follow-up, swept the new fix code adversarially, and re-ran the required suites. All probes ran read-only on the JVM lane through the test namespace's own runners (`run-outcome`, `walker-run`, `semantic-run`, `run-library`, and `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` for UAST-level construction); no source files edited, no commit made. Probe-honesty note: two of my early probe constructions were themselves broken (keyword vectors `[:go 3 0]` read as keyword calls, and `conj` on a quoted list reordering forms); the corrected runs below are the ones I stand behind, and one earlier \\\"crash\\\" (raw-entry application) was my harness passing forms to an AST runner — the corrected probe refuses cleanly.\\n\\n## Per-finding verification (s1 items, kimi r4 rulings applied)\\n\\n**P1-1 shadowable names — CLOSED.** Ruling (b): hygienic `dsg__` aliases, not binding rejection. Implemented as `dsg__get/dsg__equal/dsg__assoc/dsg__empty` bound around the whole program (clojure.cljc:1985-2040), referenced throughout `call-entry`/`admit-call-descriptor`/`compile-guest-call`; `dsg__` rejected in ANY user symbol position (check-program-symbols!, clojure.cljc:1873-1896; def names and binders separately guarded, 194-216). Probes (all fixed; each was a crash or corruption in s1): `(def f (let [get 5] (fn [x] x))) (f 1)` → `[:yin.k/ok 1]`; for each of `get = assoc empty?`: `(let [b 5] …fn…call)` → `[:yin.k/ok 21]`, `(def b 5)` then defn+call → `[:yin.k/ok 21]`, `(let [b not] …)` → `[:yin.k/ok 21]`; raise-path param shadows (`get`/`=`/`assoc`/`empty?` as the fn's parameter around a throwing callee) → `[:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message \\\"boom\\\", :yin.k/data {:x 21}}]` — the outcome survives intact.\\n\\n**P1-2 assoc-forged descriptors — CLOSED as probed; boundary re-opened at the protocol front door (N1 below).** Ruling (a): constructor/profile-produced descriptors distinguished from ordinary source maps. Admission witness: `admit-call-descriptor` (clojure.cljc:1072-1089) stores a closure captured over the canonical descriptor; `dsg__admitted?` reconstructs and compares via `data/content=` and never applies a source-supplied predicate; the gate wraps every source application (clojure.cljc:1239). Application-syntax probes: forged `(assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))` → `:yang.call/not-callable` (s1: `[:yin.k/ok 42]`); entry-swap f→g → refused; `(assoc f :extra :forged)` → refused; copy entry+admission keys → refused; `(apply h [21])` → refused; entry-spelled data with a non-callable entry stays data (`(yang.call/eligible? {:yang.call/entry 5})` → `[:yin.k/ok false]`). Negative fixtures exist (`test-common-call-source-admission`, 10 shapes × 2 wrappers). **But** the same forged map handed to `yang.call/invoke` runs — see N1.\\n\\n**P2-1 direct-apply branch — CLOSED.** Ruling (c): every source-resolved application through common admission; missing entry means `:yang.call/not-callable`. Probes: `(let [x 5] (x 1))` → `[:yin.k/error {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data {:yang.call/callee 5}}]` (s1: host crash); ordinary map and entry-less foreign value (`{:py/type :function :code :legacy}`) → same qualified condition; refusal also in tail position; non-symbol operators are gated too (`((if true f g) 21)` → 42 works, `((if false f 5) 21)` and `((yang.call/entry f) {})` → not-callable). Only generated `dsg__` implementation calls apply closures directly, as ruled.\\n\\n**P2-2 tail-chain stack growth — CLOSED.** Ruling (d): constant stack restored on BOTH lanes. Independent shapes (not the fixture's): 2-arg accumulator self-recursion `(go 1000 0)` → `[:yin.k/ok 1000]`, walker depth 1 at n=0 and n=1000, semantic depth 5 at both — constant; 3-cycle mutual chain (ma→mb→mc→ma, my shape) → `[:yin.k/ok :done]` both lanes, walker 1 / semantic 5, constant 0→1000; sensitivity control: non-tail `(grow n)` semantic depth 5 → 34 (n=10) → 304 (n=100) — the metric grows when it should (walker lane masks depth, as the fixture itself documents). The reinstated assertions (`d100 = d1000`, `≤ d0+2`, both lanes, non-tail negative control) are in `test-outcome-common-call-depth` (clojure_test.clj:1450-1484) and pass in the suite run below. Mechanism: outcome-token fast path in `call-entry` (clojure.cljc:1060-1069) and the tail `invoke-with` lane (1229-1238).\\n\\n**P3-1 continuation fallback — CLOSED.** Ruling (e): explicit refusal. Replacing `u/capture` with `:invalid-continuation` in compiled programs: operand position `(+ 1 (f 41))` and a non-tail fn body both → `[:yin.k/error {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/invalid-invocation, :data {:yang.call/reason :missing-raise-exit}}]` (clojure.cljc:1217-1227). Note the refusal only arms in non-tail bridges — tail calls use the token path — matching the fixture.\\n\\n**P3-2 named-argument classification — CLOSED (conformant per ruling (e); not re-raised, no new evidence).** Probe: `(yang.call/invoke f [] [[\\\"x\\\" 1]] raise)` → `[:yang.call/thrown \\\"Clojure functions do not accept named arguments\\\" [[\\\"x\\\" 1]]]` — reason, message and the named payload round-trip intact, as ruled.\\n\\n## Eligibility follow-up (abbfb998) — VERIFIED\\n\\n`(yang.call/eligible?)` as a source operator compiles to admitted-first-then-predicate (clojure.cljc:1286-1296); `fn?` in the guest library consults it (clojure.cljc:2146-2148). Probes via `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` (the sanctioned profile construction path), both lanes: descriptor with `:yang.call/eligible?` → `true`: call works `[:yin.k/ok :called]`; predicate `false`: call refused `:yang.call/not-callable`; operator form honors the predicate (`true`/`false`); plain source fns still admitted (`[:yin.k/ok [true 42]]`). Matches `test-common-call-profile-eligibility`. Value-position references do NOT leak a structural answer: `(def p yang.call/eligible?) (p h)` → false and library `(fn? h)` → false for the forged `h` — consistent with the gated operator form (the library defn compiles through the same branch).\\n\\n## New findings (this round)\\n\\n**P2 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1299 (native-name? branch, via :588) + src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:22,33-40 | Admission bypass at the protocol front door: `yang.call/invoke`/`invoke-with` skip the witness gate.** Namespaced operators are `native-name?`-true, so source calls to `yang.call/invoke` and `yang.call/invoke-with` compile through the direct native application branch and never pass `dsg__admitted?`; the runtime `yang.call/eligible?` they consult is structural **by design** (\\\"Descriptor projection remains structural after callable admission\\\", call.cljc:22). Probes: `(let [h (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))] (yang.call/invoke h [21] [] raise))` → **`[:yin.k/ok 42]`** — the forged callee runs (same via `invoke-with`); control with the real `f` → 42, so the surface itself is legitimate and corpus-exercised. Sharper shapes: guest-fn or scalar entries forged the same way → raw host **\\\"Cannot apply non-function\\\"** — a defect, not a condition — the exact class ruling (c) said must not surface. Mitigations found: admitted-but-ineligible profiles still refuse through the runtime predicate hook, and the copied entry only re-runs its original body (no privilege escalation demonstrated — the forged callable is behaviorally the original). Still, ruling (a) states the boundary absolutely (\\\"Copying :yang.call/entry into it must not make it callable\\\"), and the copy IS callable here. Fix (either): extend the clojure.cljc:1286 compile-branch pattern to `invoke`/`invoke-with` so their callee operand is witness-gated (smallest change: one more branch case), or record with the architect that the protocol's own front door is the sanctioned profile-level construction channel, with a ledger row plus fixtures pinning exactly what it admits and that non-closure entries refuse with a qualified condition rather than a host defect.\\n\\n**P2 | docs/design/host-boundary/closure-{clj,cljs,cljd}.edn | Red gate test: the branch's new closure edge is not in the committed closure manifests.** `dao.host.gate-test/independent-traversal-agrees-with-the-committed-closure` fails on all three lanes: `walked, not committed #{src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc}`. Cause: the adoption added yang.clojure's ns-level `(:require [yang.core.call :as call] …)` (clojure.cljc:28-30), so traversal B from `yin.repl.main` now reaches call.cljc on every lane, but no closure manifest lists it. Verified branch-caused: at `63674368` yang.clojure does not require yang.core.call (the only other requirer, the typescript prelude, is outside the closure). Outside the four required namespaces and the oracle (both green below) but a red test on the branch. Fix: regenerate the three manifests (traversal A lives in src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj) in the adoption's follow-up; totals assertions in the same file will need the new counts.\\n\\n**P3 | informational | `fn?`/value-position predicates, `conj`/`vec` defs — probed, no action.** `(def conj 5)` / `(def vec 5)` are refused at compile (\\\"would capture the argument-vector constructor\\\") — the generated helpers' bare `conj` stays safe. `(fn? h)` and `(p h)` on forged `h` answer false consistently with the gated operator.\\n\\n## Independent suite re-verification (exact counts, JVM)\\n\\n- Four required (engineer brief): `yang.clojure-test` **85/763**, `yang.core.call-test` **4/13**, `yin.vm.macro-test` **25/188**, `yang.clojure.stream-eval-test` **13/309** — total **127 tests / 1273 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (matches the fix2 report exactly; the known-gaps ledger's 126/1267 predates abbfb998's added regression, which reconciles the two).\\n- Accumulated LaTTe oracle: `yang.latte-oracle-test` **21 tests / 971 assertions, 0 failures**.\\n- Bonus regression: `yang.python.antlr.call-entry-test` **1/4, 0 failures**.\\n- Kondo could not be independently re-run in this sandbox (`clojure -M:kondo` approval denied); the engineer's 0-errors/2-pre-existing-oracle-warnings claim stands unverified by me.\\n- Broader JVM suite (accidental full run via `-M:test`): reached dao.host.gate-test with only the N2 three-lane failure before the run was cut at its time limit; everything before it green.\\n\\n## CLOSED/OPEN table\\n\\n| Item | Ruling | Verdict |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| P1-1 shadowable names capture generated code | (b) dsg__ aliases | **CLOSED** (all 17 probes) |\\n| P1-2 assoc-forged descriptors | (a) admission witness | **CLOSED as probed** — boundary re-opened at `yang.call/invoke` (new P2, N1) |\\n| P2-1 direct-apply too broad | (c) admission for source calls | **CLOSED** (qualified refusal everywhere probed, incl. non-symbol operators) |\\n| P2-2 tail-chain stack growth | (d) constant stack both lanes | **CLOSED** (independent shapes + reinstated assertions green) |\\n| P3-1 continuation fallback | (e) explicit refusal | **CLOSED** (`:yang.call/invalid-invocation` + `:missing-raise-exit`) |\\n| P3-2 named-arg classification | (e) conformant, not re-raised | **CLOSED (conformant)** — payload round-trip intact |\\n| Eligibility follow-up (abbfb998) | abbfb998 | **VERIFIED** (predicate gates calls both lanes; plain fns unaffected) |\\n| N1 invoke/invoke-with witness bypass | new, needs architect | **OPEN** (P2) |\\n| N2 closure manifests missing call.cljc | new, repo hygiene | **OPEN** (P2; red gate test, all 3 lanes) |\\n\\n## Sign-off readiness\\n\\n**Not ready yet — two P2s from this round, both small and well-bounded.** All six arbitrated items are closed with probe evidence and the four required namespaces + the oracle corpus are green at exactly the reported counts, so the r4 fix work itself is confirmed. Before Architect sign-off: (1) N1 needs an architect decision — gate `yang.call/invoke`/`invoke-with` callees at the compile branch like `yang.call/eligible?`, or record the front door as the sanctioned construction channel with a ledger row and refusal fixtures (including qualified refusal, not a host crash, for non-closure forged entries); (2) N2 needs the three closure manifests regenerated. If the architect accepts N1 as a recorded deviation with fixtures, the round can proceed to sign-off with N2's manifest regeneration as the only code-side touch.\\n\\n---\\n\\nExecution summary: probes A1–A6, B, C1–C4 ran via one-line `clojure -M -e` against the loaded test namespace; suites ran via `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. One background full-suite run (accidental, via `-M:test -e`) was cut at its time limit after surfacing the N2 gate failures — reported as observed, not completed. No files in the repo were modified and nothing was committed; the report above is the deliverable since collab/ writes were denied.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793392000000-architect-adopt-signoff.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:26:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,110p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && sed -n '560,605p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1130,1175p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1260,1338p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1860,1957p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\n```\\n\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\n;;           (let [a (data/nth dsg__av 0)] e1)\\n;;           (if (data/content= (data/count dsg__av) 2)\\n;;             (let [a (data/nth dsg__av 0) b (data/nth dsg__av 1)] e2)\\n;;             (data/count))))                     ; refused: no such arity\\n;;\\n;; The dispatch names are module-qualified, which no let binder can shadow.\\n;; A call is (f (conj [] a b)) in tail position when the operator is not\\n;; statically native; a native name (a primitive or module export, not bound\\n;; locally and not defined by the program) is called directly, and in value\\n;; position becomes a fresh adapter closure over the same convention. The\\n;; program's own macro names stay ordinary applications: the expander\\n;; recognizes those calls, so their operands must stay ordinary.\\n\\n(def ^:private av-sym (symbol (str reserved-prefix \\\"av\\\")))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private default-adapter-arity\\n  \\\"The largest count an adapter admits for a native name with no declared\\n   profile (`:native` given as a plain set).\\\"\\n  4)\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private adapter-variadic-cap\\n  \\\"How far an adapter extends a `:variadic` profile arity: an adapter is a\\n   finite dispatch, so an open-ended arity needs a stated bound.\\\"\\n  8)\\n\\n\\n(defn- native-name?\\n  \\\"True when `sym` statically names a primitive or host-module export.\\\"\\n  [env sym]\\n  (when-let [conv (::convention env)]\\n    (and (symbol? sym)\\n         (not (contains? (locals env) sym))\\n         (not (contains? (::defined env) sym))\\n         (or (some? (namespace sym))\\n             (contains? (:native conv) sym)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- macro-call?\\n  [env macro-env sym]\\n  (and (symbol? sym)\\n       (not (contains? (locals env) sym))\\n       (or (contains? macro-env sym)\\n           (contains? (:macros (::convention env)) sym))))\\n\\n\\n    :else (reject! \\\"fn parameters must be a vector\\\" {:params (first operands)})))\\n\\n\\n(defn- adapter-counts\\n  \\\"The argument counts an adapter for native `sym` admits: the fixed arities\\n   of its `:yin.k/arities` profile, extended to `adapter-variadic-cap` when\\n   the profile is `:variadic`. A name with no declared profile admits\\n   0 to `default-adapter-arity`.\\\"\\n  [env sym]\\n  (if-let [declared (get-in env [::convention :arities sym])]\\n    (let [fixed (filter number? declared)\\n          from (if (seq fixed) (apply max fixed) 0)]\\n      (vec (sort (distinct (concat fixed\\n                                   (when (some #{:variadic} declared)\\n                                     (range from (inc (max from adapter-variadic-cap)))))))))\\n    (vec (range (inc default-adapter-arity)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- compile-adapter\\n  \\\"A fresh closure over the convention that calls native `sym` with the\\n   unpacked arguments, for each admitted count.\\\"\\n  [sym env tail? macro-env]\\n  (let [arities (mapv (fn [n]\\n                        (let [call (cons sym (map #(list 'data/nth av-sym %) (range n)))]\\n                          {:n n\\n                           :form (if (outcomes? env)\\n                                   (lower-function-body env macro-env [] [call])\\n                                   call)}))\\n                      (adapter-counts env sym))]\\n    (compile-lambda [av-sym] (av-dispatch env sym nil arities) env tail? macro-env)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- apply-call?\\n  \\\"True when `operator` is the `apply` intrinsic: not bound locally and not\\n   defined by the program.\\\"\\n  [env operator]\\n  (and (= 'apply operator)\\n       (not (contains? (locals env) operator))\\n       (not (contains? (::defined env) operator))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- compile-guest-call\\n  \\\"Admit a source operator before common invocation. Only generated UAST\\n   implementation calls apply private closures directly.\\\"\\n  [callee-ast arguments-ast env tail?]\\n                                        coll-form)\\n                                      false env macro-env)\\n                        env tail?)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- compile-av-application\\n  [operator operands env tail? macro-env]\\n  (cond\\n    (apply-call? env operator) (compile-apply operands env tail? macro-env)\\n\\n    ;; (:k m) / (:k m default): a static lookup\\n    (keyword? operator)\\n    (do (when-not (<= 1 (count operands) 2)\\n          (reject! \\\"A keyword call takes a map and an optional default\\\"\\n                   {:keyword operator, :operands (count operands)}))\\n        (when (contains? (locals env) 'get)\\n          (reject! \\\"A local binding of get would capture a keyword lookup\\\"\\n                   {:keyword operator}))\\n        (cond-> {:type :application\\n                 :operator {:type :variable, :name 'get}\\n                 :operands (into [(compile-form (first operands) false env macro-env)\\n                                  (compile-literal operator false)]\\n                                 (map #(compile-form % false env macro-env))\\n                                 (rest operands))}\\n          tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\\n\\n    (and (outcomes? env) (= operator 'yang.call/eligible?))\\n    (let [candidate 'dsg__eligibility_candidate]\\n      (cond-> (u/mark-tails\\n                (apply u/app\\n                       (u/lam [candidate]\\n                              (u/if-node\\n                                (u/app (u/v 'dsg__admitted?) (u/v candidate))\\n                                (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/eligible?) (u/v candidate))\\n                                (u/lit false)))\\n                       (map #(compile-form % false env macro-env) operands)))\\n        tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\\n\\n    (and (outcomes? env)\\n         (#{'yang.call/invoke 'yang.call/invoke-with} operator)\\n         (<= 4 (count operands)))\\n    (let [candidate 'dsg__invocation_candidate\\n          compile-operand #(compile-form % false env macro-env)]\\n      (cond-> (u/mark-tails\\n                (u/app\\n                  (u/lam [candidate]\\n                         (u/if-node\\n                           (u/app (u/v 'dsg__admitted?) (u/v candidate))\\n                           (apply u/app (u/v operator) (u/v candidate)\\n                                  (map compile-operand (rest operands)))\\n                           (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/transfer)\\n                                  (compile-operand (nth operands 3))\\n                                  (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/condition)\\n                                         (u/lit :yang.call/not-callable)\\n                                         (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/lit {})\\n                                                (u/lit :yang.call/callee)\\n                                                (u/v candidate))))))\\n                  (compile-operand (first operands))))\\n        tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\\n\\n    ;; a statically native name: call it directly\\n    (native-name? env operator)\\n    (cond-> {:type :application\\n             :operator (cond-> {:type :variable, :name operator}\\n                         (seq (meta operator)) (with-meta (meta operator)))\\n             :operands (mapv #(compile-form % false env macro-env) operands)}\\n      tail? (assoc :tail? true))\\n\\n    :else\\n    (compile-guest-call (compile-form operator false env macro-env)\\n                        (compile-form (vec operands) false env macro-env)\\n                        env tail?)))\\n\\n\\n(defn compile-application\\n  \\\"Compile a function application to Universal AST.\\n\\n  Clojure: (+ 1 2)\\n  AST: {:type :application\\n   declares domain failures to the host adapter returning its outcome.\\\"\\n  [forms opts]\\n  (if-not opts\\n    {}\\n    (do (when-not (= :arg-vector (:convention opts))\\n          (reject! \\\"Unknown compile-program convention\\\" {:opts opts}))\\n        (when-let [hit (seq (filter control-heads (:macros opts)))]\\n          (reject! \\\"A :macros entry names a compiler form\\\" {:macros (vec hit)}))\\n        (let [defined (into #{}\\n                            (keep (fn [f]\\n                                    (when (and (seq? f)\\n                                               (contains? #{'def 'defn 'defn- 'defmacro} (first f))\\n                                               (symbol? (second f)))\\n                                      (strip-symbol-meta (second f)))))\\n                            forms)]\\n          ;; the compiler emits bare conj and vec (collection literals, apply),\\n          ;; so a program definition of either would capture them\\n          (when-let [hit (first (filter #{'conj 'vec} defined))]\\n            (reject! (str \\\"Defining \\\" hit \\\" would capture the argument-vector constructor\\\")\\n                     {:name hit}))\\n          (cond-> {::convention {:native (set (if (map? (:native opts))\\n                                                (keys (:native opts))\\n                                                (:native opts)))\\n                                 :arities (into {}\\n                                                (keep (fn [[sym profile]]\\n                                                        (when-let [a (if (map? profile)\\n                                                                       (:yin.k/arities profile)\\n                                                                       profile)]\\n                                                          [sym a])))\\n                                                (when (map? (:native opts)) (:native opts)))\\n                                 :macros (set (:macros opts))}\\n                   ::defined defined}\\n            (:outcomes opts) (assoc ::outcomes {:adapters (or (:adapters opts) {})}))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- check-program-symbols!\\n  \\\"The program pre-pass. Quoted data is exempt. The reserved prefix names\\n   compiler temporaries, so no user symbol may carry it in ANY position\\n   (binder, reference, definition name); `loop` and `recur` are special forms,\\n   so they may only head a list, never stand as a value or a binder.\\\"\\n  [form]\\n  (cond\\n    (symbol? form)\\n    (do (when (reserved-name? form)\\n          (reject! \\\"Symbol uses the reserved destructuring prefix\\\"\\n                   {:symbol form, :prefix reserved-prefix}))\\n        (when (and (nil? (namespace form)) (contains? #{'loop 'recur} form))\\n          (reject! (str form \\\" is only legal in operator position\\\") {:symbol form})))\\n    (seq? form)\\n    (when-not (= 'quote (first form))\\n      (let [head (first form)]\\n        (when (and (symbol? head) (reserved-name? head))\\n          (reject! \\\"Symbol uses the reserved destructuring prefix\\\"\\n                   {:symbol head, :prefix reserved-prefix}))\\n        (when-not (symbol? head)\\n          (check-program-symbols! head))\\n        (run! check-program-symbols! (rest form))))\\n    (map? form) (run! check-program-symbols! (concat (keys form) (vals form)))\\n    (coll? form) (run! check-program-symbols! form)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- check-top-level-discard!\\n  \\\"Only the last top-level form's outcome reaches the harness, so a non-final,\\n   non-definition form that may fail would have its error silently discarded:\\n   refuse it, as a def value that calls guest functions is refused. Returns\\n   `form`.\\\"\\n  [env macro-env forms form]\\n  (when (and (next forms)\\n             (not (and (seq? form) (contains? #{'def 'defn 'defn- 'defmacro} (first form))))\\n             (failing? (assoc env ::macro-env macro-env) (rewrite-ex-info form)))\\n    (reject! \\\"A non-final top-level form that may fail would discard its error outcome\\\"\\n             {:form form}))\\n  form)\\n\\n\\n(defn- lower-top-form\\n  \\\"Under the outcome model a top-level expression returns an outcome like a\\n   function body; definitions keep their own lowering.\\\"\\n  [env macro-env form]\\n  (if (and (seq? form) (contains? #{'def 'defn 'defn- 'defmacro} (first form)))\\n    form\\n    (lower-function-body env macro-env [] [form])))\\n\\n\\n(defn compile-program\\n  \\\"Compile a sequence of Clojure forms into a single Universal AST.\\n   Top-level defs compile to explicit yin/def store writes, sequenced via lambda.\\n   Top-level defmacros are lowered to ordinary (yin/def name (fn ...)) syntax\\n   with :macro? true on the lambda; the encoder turns that flag into a\\n   declaration row and the expander, not this compiler, recognizes calls.\\n   The three-argument form takes `opts` opting into the argument-vector\\n   convention (see `program-env`); without it the output is the native one.\\\"\\n  ([forms] (compile-program forms true))\\n  ([forms tail?] (compile-program forms tail? nil))\\n  ([forms tail? opts]\\n   (run! check-program-symbols! forms)\\n   (let [env (program-env forms opts)]\\n     ;; A discarded definition must not become a lexical binding in the\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793830000000-engineer-h3-1-blocked-fixture-diagnosis.prompt.md:47:Run the focused gate test (`clojure -M:test -v yang.latte-oracle-test/test-kernel-differential-gate`) before and after your diagnostic harness changes (if any) to show no regression: before = exactly 1 failure (the blocked assertion); after = the same, unless your packet explains a difference. clj-kondo on any touched file: 0 errors. The report goes in `docs/` beside the gate records or the round findings file — state the root-cause hypothesis ranked by evidence, the four-cell table, the trial results, and the recommended next round (harness fix / compiler fix / budget proposal / shared-runtime ruling request).\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:13:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs twice daily on the remote hosts (03:00/17:00 VN);\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\n```\\n\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\n\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\n\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\n\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\n\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\n\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\n\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\navailable. **Never run the slow tier, `bb test:all`, or `nightly.clj` on the\\nlaptop** — the slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM processes each\\nmulti-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core count); four of them\\nGC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over 100 on 2026-11-01 while\\nmaking no progress (the remote hosts run this tier: jupiter 5,645 s at 12\\nworkers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane set concurrently on the\\nlaptop either.\\n\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\n\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\n\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\n\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\n\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>`. Fix against the failing test alone,\\n   re-run it green, then climb back up (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \\\"Cadence\\\" below), so a\\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-01):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\\n  including the slow tier, runs **twice a day: 03:00 and 17:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** on the\\n  remote hosts (`src/dev/nightly.clj`; 03:00 catches the day's work before the owner\\n  stops coding, 17:00 gives a result they can act on before sleeping). The measured long\\n  set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be a per-merge cost; a\\n  break is instead caught by the next scheduled run, at most ~12 hours later.\\n  The schedule is expressed as an hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard inside the\\n  driver, so it is immune to the DST shifts of either host's local timezone.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\\n\\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\\ntree that did not contain them.\\n\\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\\ngit fetch -q origin \\\"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\\"\\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\\n```\\n\\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\"`). If\\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\\n\\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\\n\\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\\nfirst lane:\\n\\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\\n   only (\\\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\\\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\\n   (2026-11-02). A fix round is in flight on `fix/gen-antlr-prereq-heuristic`.\\n\\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\\n\\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\\nchange to a ledger file:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\\n```\\n\\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\\nstaging.\\n\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\n\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\n5-6-hour run.\\n\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\n\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\n\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \\\"a second UDP bind on\\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\\\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\n\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\nchanges\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\"it should not be for\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\"\\n\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\n\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-full.sh` on\\n  shiva, tmux session `nightly`, scheduled 03:00 daily through the\\n  orchestrator's automation; it fetches and checks out origin/master\\n  before anything runs). Every lane, every host target: the JVM full\\n  suite, the full Node suite, the full Dart suite. Its results are a\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\n\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\n\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\n\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\n\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\n\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\n\\\"Patch does not apply\\\" or \\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\".\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\n\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\n\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\"run tests remotely\\nover SSH\\\" to \\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\": a heavy implementation or\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\n\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\nreference):\\n\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\"Get the code there\\\".\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\"$(mise env -s\\n   bash)\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\n   inside the same command.\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\n\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:18:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'ls lib/antlr && command -v clojure && command -v clj-kondo'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:20:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'ls lib/antlr && command -v clojure && command -v clj-kondo'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"ls: lib/antlr: No such file or directory\\n\",\"exit_code\":1,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:21:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h31/collab/yang-clojure/1794100000000-engineer-h3-1-wedge-round2.glm-5.3.findings.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# H3-1 wedge diagnostic, round 2 — the findings report\\n\\nCreated: 2026-11-04 (GMT) · Agent: glm (glm-5.3) · Branch: `hardening3-h3-1` at `8ea857c1`\\nTask: BRIEF-wedge.md (per the architect's continuation ruling, steps 3–4)\\nArtifacts: `test/d2_wedge_probe2.clj` (stages A–D, F + gate stage C),\\n`test/d2_wedge_min_repro.clj` (arms A–E); evidence logs in this directory\\n(`1794100000000-engineer-h3-1-wedge.probe2-run1|-gate1|-walker1|-walker8m|.minrepro.log`)\\n\\n## Verdict in one paragraph\\n\\nThere is **no non-terminating loop in the encoder session layer, the observer\\ncoordination, or the semantic evaluator** — and the kernel-c semantic path\\ncontains no encoder session at any commit. The round-1 \\\"evaluator terminates /\\nwedge lives in the encoder session layer\\\" split was an artifact of a vacuous\\nprobe. The gate stall that motivated this round is real, it reproduces, and it\\nlives **after** the walker cell's legitimate budget exhaustion: the gate's\\n`kernel-attempt` hands the exhaustion exception's `:consumer` — the entire\\nadvanced machine — to `evidence-data` → `upstream-record-data`, whose\\n`clojure.walk/postwalk` re-expands it with five `requiring-resolve` calls per\\nnode and no cycle guard. That host-side walk, not any VM loop, is what burns\\n2400s+ with zero guest steps. D2's real regression is smaller and factual: it\\npushed this fixture's **walker** cell over the 2M budget (the semantic cell\\nstill completes at 1.1M).\\n\\n## 1. Round 1's probe was vacuous — the layer-location built on it is retracted\\n\\n`test/d2_wedge_probe.clj` builds `semantic/create-vm` + `tu/make-observer-session`\\nand calls `observer/run-on-stream` — but **never queues the fixture onto the\\nmedium** (no `tu/queue-rows!` anywhere in the file). An observer session over\\nan empty medium observes nothing (`:retry`/`:ended`), never calls `load`, never\\nenters `run`. And a fresh `SemanticVM` is minted `:halted? true`\\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:432`), so the untouched machine reads \\\"halted\\\".\\n\\nMeasured (stage A, `…-probe2-run1.log` in this directory):\\n\\n```\\n:STAGE-A-RESULT {:steps 0, :loads 0, :halted true, :blocked false,\\n                 :verdict :VACUOUS-no-program-ever-loaded}\\n```\\n\\nZero steps. The \\\"terminates, halted, well under 2M\\\" measurement in commit\\n`8ea857c1` measured a machine that never executed an instruction.\\n\\nThe same commit message's premise — \\\"kernel-c wraps the machine in\\ntu/make-encoder-session + encoder/forward-on-stream\\\" — is false at `8ea857c1`\\nand at every commit since `run-guest-machine` was introduced (`7723c6ae`): the\\nkernel-c path is `tu/make-observer-session` + `tu/queue-rows!`/`queue-ast!` +\\none `observer/run-on-stream` call (`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1995-2009`),\\nsingle stage, no encoder. The only encoder references in `clojure_test.clj`\\nare the descriptor tests (lines 785, 1028, 1047).\\n\\n## 2. The real kernel-c semantic cell completes (stage B)\\n\\nDriving the exact kernel-c semantic call —\\n`run-guest-machine ast (oracle-registry yang/kind-module) :semantic 2000000` —\\ninstrumented at `vm/step` and at the `run-on-stream` boundary:\\n\\n```\\n:WORKER-DONE {:kind :returned,\\n              :steps 1105792, :in-step-ms 2535, :loads 1, :ready-checks 3,\\n              :runs [{:entry-steps 0, :exit-steps 1105792, :threw false}],\\n              :halted true, :blocked false,\\n              :value [:yin.k/ok [:ok [(<a>) (Π [A ✳] (Π [B ✳] …))]]]}\\n```\\n\\n1,105,792 steps (55% of budget), 2.5 s of stepping, correct proof outcome. This\\nmatches the pre-D2 measurement (`0dc54289`: both fixtures complete inside 2M\\nafter the call-lowering fix) — D2 did not make the semantic cell explode.\\n\\n## 3. The observer/encoder layer cannot be the loop (code + measurement)\\n\\nThe brief's candidate — the budget throw caught by `carry-session` and fed back\\nvia `consumer-left-by-run` — does not exist:\\n\\n- `carry-session` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:211-214`) **rethrows**:\\n  `(throw (ex-info … (assoc (ex-data e) :session session) e))`. Both catch\\n  sites in `run-on-stream` (`observer.cljc:250-255` and `277-282`) call it\\n  directly; neither recurs.\\n- `consumer-left-by-run` (`observer.cljc:183-196`) only reads `:consumer` from\\n  the exception's data to attach to the carried `:session`. It is never a loop\\n  variable; no caller re-enters `run` with it inside this harness.\\n- `run-on-stream`'s loop re-enters only on `:advance` (a batch observed), `:gap`\\n  (recovery cursor), or not-ready→run→ready. This harness queues exactly one\\n  batch: measured `loads 1, runs 1`.\\n- Arm C of the minimized reproduction drives the semantic cell at a 10,000-step\\n  budget: the exhaustion throw **propagates out** of `run-on-stream` with\\n  `:consumer` (advanced machine), `:session` (partial session), and the cause\\n  preserved. Recovery is opt-in data for callers; there is no retry cycle.\\n\\n## 4. Where the gate actually stalls — the exact site, caught in the act\\n\\nStage C drove the full 106-fixture differential gate\\n(`test-kernel-differential-gate`) under the same instrumentation with a\\nfreeze detector. It froze mid-corpus, in `check-assume-have-discharge`\\n(fixture 50 of 106), **inside the walker C cell** — the opposite lane from the\\nD2 report's attribution:\\n\\n```\\n:LEG :a …wall-ms 5      (check-assume-have-discharge)\\n:LEG :b …wall-ms 89\\n:LEG :compile …wall-ms 51\\n:C-ENTER {:lane :walker, :at-steps 3786531, :total-s 91}\\n   … steps advance to 5786531 (= 3786531 + exactly 2000000) in ~20 s …\\n   … then zero steps for ≥300 s; no :C-EXIT ever prints; detector fired\\n```\\n\\nThe captured worker stack (top frames, full dump in\\n`1794100000000-engineer-h3-1-wedge.probe2-gate1.log` in this directory):\\n\\n```\\nclojure.lang.Var.getThreadBinding → requiring-resolve\\nyang.latte_oracle_test$upstream_fn.invokeStatic        (latte_oracle_test.clj:286)\\nyang.latte_oracle_test$upstream_record_data$…invoke    (latte_oracle_test.clj:1025)\\nclojure.walk$postwalk → into → reduce → LazySeq.force → clojure.walk$postwalk …\\n```\\n\\nThe causal chain, with line references:\\n\\n1. `run-bounded` (`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1987-1993`) counts 2,000,000\\n   walker steps — the walker cell of this fixture now **exhausts** the budget\\n   (it completed inside 2M before D2) — and throws\\n   `\\\"Guest instruction budget exhausted\\\"` with `{:lane … :steps … :consumer v}`\\n   (`clojure_test.clj:1991-1992`; the `:consumer` addition is the H3-1 fix\\n   round's own one-line harness change).\\n2. `run-on-stream`'s run-catch (`observer.cljc:277-282`) wraps via\\n   `carry-session` — a cheap rethrow adding `:session`.\\n3. `kernel-c`'s body runs inside `kernel-attempt`\\n   (`test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1659-1671`), whose catch builds\\n   `:data (evidence-data (dissoc (ex-data e) :session))` — it strips `:session`\\n   but **not `:consumer`**, so the entire advanced machine enters\\n   `evidence-data`.\\n4. `evidence-data` (`latte_oracle_test.clj:1645-1656`) hits `(record? x)` — the\\n   machine is a record — and calls `upstream-record-data`\\n   (`latte_oracle_test.clj:1014-1028`): a `clojure.walk/postwalk` over the\\n   whole machine that calls `(upstream-fn pred)` — i.e.\\n   `requiring-resolve` (`latte_oracle_test.clj:284-286`) — **five times per\\n   node**, then `evidence-data` recurses into every child, and every child\\n   record (each VM `Closure`/`Continuation` is a record) triggers another full\\n   `postwalk` of its subtree.\\n\\nThat is the \\\"wedge\\\": an unbounded, zero-guest-step, host-side expansion of the\\nexhausted machine. Whether it is truly non-terminating (a cyclic closure\\nenvironment — recursive guest functions whose captured env binds their own\\nclosure record, on which `postwalk`, which has no seen-set, never terminates)\\nor merely hours-scale (per-record re-walk of shared env chains × five\\n`requiring-resolve` per node) is settled by the cycle scan in §6.\\n\\nThis also re-explains the D2-era observations without the encoder theory: the\\n2400 s/5400 s \\\"semantic cell wall-clock explosion\\\" runs were this same\\npost-exhaustion expansion, mid-corpus, with no per-cell attribution recorded\\n(\\\"guest-step counts NOT yet captured\\\" in `805d81d6`'s message).\\n\\n## 5. The exhausted walker cell in isolation (stage D) — bounded state capture\\n\\nSame driver, walker lane, alone (no corpus context):\\n\\n```\\n:WORKER-DONE {:kind :threw, :message \\\"Guest instruction budget exhausted\\\",\\n              :steps 2000000, :in-step-ms 10591, :loads 1,\\n              :runs [{:entry-steps 0, :exit-steps 2000000,\\n                      :threw \\\"Guest instruction budget exhausted\\\"}],\\n              :data-keys [:lane :steps :consumer :session],\\n              :consumer {:halted? false, :blocked? false, :cont-depth 1,\\n                         :control {}, :wait-set 0, :ready-queue 0,\\n                         :value [\\\"_\\\" 6]}}\\n```\\n\\nThe exhaustion itself is fast (≈11 s) and well-behaved: the throw propagates,\\n`:consumer`/`:session` ride along exactly as the recovery design intends. The\\nbrief's requested capture at wedge time: the advanced consumer is **neither\\nhalted nor blocked**, continuation depth 1, mid-walk control, gensym value\\n`[\\\"_\\\" 6]` — the walker died inside fresh-supply generation under the proof\\nloop, not in any stream or admission machinery.\\n\\n## 6. Minimized reproduction — `test/d2_wedge_min_repro.clj`\\n\\nFive arms, each runnable alone (`clojure -M:test -n d2-wedge-min-repro -v\\nd2-wedge-min-repro/test-h31-wedge-min-repro`, ≈4–6 min):\\n\\n- **arm A** — round 1's drive, verbatim (session, no queue):\\n  `{0 steps, halted true}` — the false negative in three lines.\\n- **arm B** — the same session plus the one omitted line (`tu/queue-rows!`):\\n  completes at **1,105,792 steps**, halted, correct outcome.\\n- **arm C** — semantic at a 10,000-step budget: the throw escapes\\n  `run-on-stream` carrying `:consumer` + `:session`; recovery is opt-in.\\n- **arm D** — the gate freeze mechanism, isolated: drive the walker cell to its\\n  exhaustion throw, then time exactly kernel-attempt's\\n  `(evidence-data (dissoc ex-data :session))` under a 120 s cap with a stack\\n  dump. Result: **unfinished after the 120 s cap** — stack shows the same\\n  `clojure.walk$postwalk` recursion the gate freeze showed. The walker drive\\n  itself took ≈11 s; the post-throw expansion is >10× that and still running\\n  when capped.\\n- **arm E** — an identity-based DFS cycle scan over the exhausted `:consumer`\\n  (the same shapes `evidence-data`/`postwalk` traverse), reporting unique\\n  container count, off-path sharing, and whether any node re-enters the DFS\\n  path (a true cycle ⇒ `postwalk` never terminates). Result:\\n  `{:unique-containers 78913, :shared-revisits 3442, :cycle nil}` — **no\\n  cycle**. The machine is a finite DAG, so the walk terminates *eventually*;\\n  its cost is the re-expansion: every record child (each VM Closure/\\n  Continuation) triggers `upstream-record-data`'s own full postwalk of its\\n  subtree, and the 3,442 sharing points make those subtrees overlap —\\n  finite-but-astronomical, which is exactly the shape \\\"2400 s × 2 stalled,\\n  5400 s never returned\\\" describes: not an infinite loop, an expansion that\\n  had not finished inside the trial window.\\n\\n## 7. How far over the budget is the walker? (stage F — bounded trial)\\n\\nAt an 8,000,000-step budget the walker cell **completes at 2,024,912 steps**\\n(11.8 s in-step) with the same correct outcome as the semantic cell:\\n\\n```\\n:WORKER-DONE {:kind :returned, :steps 2024912, :halted true,\\n              :value [:yin.k/ok [:ok [(<a>) (Π [A ✳] …)]]]}\\n```\\n\\nThe walker misses the 2M budget by **24,912 steps — 1.2%**. D2's per-call\\npacket dispatch pushed a cell that previously completed inside 2M (commit\\n`0dc54289`) just barely over, and the harness's post-exhaustion expansion\\nturned that near-miss into the multi-hour \\\"wedge\\\". The semantic cell needs\\n1,105,792 (55% of budget).\\n\\n## 8. Recommendation\\n\\n1. **No yin.vm / observer / encoder architectural ruling is needed.** Nothing\\n   in `src/cljc/**` non-terminates or misbehaves; the observer recovery\\n   protocol did exactly what it is designed to do. No shared-runtime change is\\n   requested.\\n2. **The gate stall is a one-line harness defect** in\\n   `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1667`: `kernel-attempt`'s catch feeds the\\n   exhaustion's `:consumer` machine to `evidence-data`. The comment above that\\n   very line already classifies `:session` as \\\"diagnostic machinery, not a\\n   portable domain payload; recursively expanding its closure environments\\n   duplicates the whole program\\\" — `:consumer` is the same class of payload.\\n   Proposed next-round fix: `(dissoc (ex-data e) :session :consumer)`, or\\n   replace `:consumer` with a bounded snapshot (halted/blocked/cont-depth/\\n   control) before expansion. latte_oracle_test.clj is out of bounds THIS\\n   round by the brief, so this is a recommendation, not a change.\\n3. **The D2 landing decision should be re-based on these numbers:** semantic\\n   cell 1,105,792 steps (completes); walker cell >2,000,000 (exhausts; see §7\\n   for the true count). The \\\"wall-clock explosion\\\" was the harness amplifying\\n   the walker's exhaustion, but the exhaustion itself is D2's per-call packet\\n   dispatch cost on the walker lane. Whatever the landing round decides\\n   (budget raise is DEFERRED by ruling; trimming the dispatch is a compiler\\n   change requiring its own scope), it no longer needs to explain a semantic\\n   wedge.\\n4. **Correct the record:** commit `8ea857c1`'s conclusion line (\\\"the wedge\\n   lives in the encoder session layer\\\") should be superseded by this report;\\n   `test/d2_wedge_probe.clj` should either gain its missing queue line or be\\n   retired in favor of `d2_wedge_probe2.clj` (this round leaves it untouched —\\n   it is the previous round's committed artifact and the kimi gate reviews\\n   before landing).\\n\\n## Reproduction ladder (all at `8ea857c1`, JVM 17, -Xss64m, this worktree)\\n\\n| run | command | outcome |\\n|-----|---------|---------|\\n| stage A+B | `clojure -M:test -n d2-wedge-probe2 -v d2-wedge-probe2/test-h31-wedge-probe2` | A vacuous (0 steps); B returns at 1,105,792 steps |\\n| stage C (gate) | `clojure -M:test -n d2-wedge-probe2 -v d2-wedge-probe2/test-h31-wedge-probe2-gate` | frozen mid-corpus in the walker cell of check-assume-have-discharge; stack = evidence-data/postwalk; exit 3 by design |\\n| stage D (walker isolated) | `… -v d2-wedge-probe2/test-h31-wedge-probe2-walker` | throws at exactly 2,000,000 steps in ≈11 s |\\n| stage F (8M trial) | `… -v d2-wedge-probe2/test-h31-wedge-probe2-walker-8m` | returns at 2,024,912 steps (1.2% over the 2M budget) |\\n| min-repro A–E | `clojure -M:test -n d2-wedge-min-repro -v d2-wedge-min-repro/test-h31-wedge-min-repro` | A 0 steps; B 1,105,792; C throw propagates; D >120 s unfinished (stack = postwalk); E 78,913 containers, 3,442 shared, no cycle |\\n\\nclj-kondo on both new files: 0 errors, 0 warnings (run through the lane's\\nProcessBuilder shim, deleted after).\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:27:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'command -v clojure; command -v clj-kondo; git log -1 --oneline'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:28:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'command -v clojure; command -v clj-kondo; git log -1 --oneline'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/opt/homebrew/bin/clojure\\ned4ca1dc docs(log): the relay routed — the clojure runtime-module round dispatched to muse (shell-capable)\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:37:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'kondo|antlr' bb.edn deps.edn | head -60\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794210000000-engineer-evidence-walk-fix.codex.stdout.log:38:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'kondo|antlr' bb.edn deps.edn | head -60\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"deps.edn:12:        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\\ndeps.edn:13:        ;;